=== Introduction
--- Dark Sayings upon the Harp [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 49
sub: The futility of riches • God will redeem my soul • Dark sayings made plain
refs: Psalm 49:4; Psalm 49:15; Psalm 49:20
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse. Press C for the whole chapter.

=== Riches and the grave
--- Hear this, all ye people
kicker: Psalm 49 · Rich and poor
scripture: Psalm 49:1-4 | mark: Both low and high, rich and poor, together; I will open my dark saying upon the harp
p: Psalm 49 is a meditation on the futility of life, in the building of riches, honor and name, when death awaits everyone, rich or poor, wise or fool.
p: This reflection on life's vanities ties to Solomon's writings in **Ecclesiastes**, from the call to harken to the preacher to the vanity of life and the only remedy, fearing the Lord and receiving His blessing.

--- How dieth the wise man?
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 49:10
big: Ecclesiastes 2:16 | mark: And how dieth the wise man? as the fool
p: This echoes the same sentiment found in verse 10;
big: Psalm 49:10
big: Ecclesiastes 5:15 | mark: naked shall he return to go as he came

--- Get wisdom
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 49:3–4
p: Solomon learned at the feet of his father, and after careful study of all the things his wealth and power availed him of, with the wisdom above other men, granted by God's grace, he would come to the same conclusions David sang of.
p: The call to incline your ear to hear wisdom, to listen to the dark sayings and parables. To seek understanding is a major theme of Solomon's **Proverbs**;
big: Proverbs 4:7 | mark: Wisdom is the principal thing
p: David teaches us the same lessons in Psalms. The wisdom of God is most valuable, because in that knowledge we may find eternal life.

--- The iniquity of my heels
kicker: Psalm 49 · Wherefore should I fear?
big: Psalm 49:5
p: David expresses confidence in his salvation. He is not afraid when the time comes to be given the wages of his sin, the iniquity of his heels, the death that awaits us all, the consequences of our own making.
p: Why should we fear in the days of evil, that comes our own making? He knows his redemption is a gift of God. But the man trusting his riches has no hope at all in that day. They cannot redeem themselves or their brothers, and cannot pay the ransom due God for their sins.

--- None can redeem his brother
kicker: Psalm 49 · The ransom
scripture: Psalm 49:6-9 | mark: None of them can by any means redeem his brother, nor give to God a ransom for him
p: Again, this idea that man's riches are worthless in the judgment, that they do nothing for the dead, is repeated by Solomon;
big: Proverbs 11:4 | mark: Riches profit not in the day of wrath
big: Proverbs 27:24 | mark: For riches are not for ever

--- This their way is their folly
kicker: Psalm 49 · Like the beasts that perish
scripture: Psalm 49:11-14 | mark: This their way is their folly
p: Their way is folly. The wise man dies like the fool, like the brute, and all their wealth is left for others. But the precious redemption of the soul, that one lives forever and shall not see corruption, that is of God, through faith in the Lord Christ Jesus.
big: 1 Timothy 2:5-6 | mark: Who gave himself a ransom for all

--- He shall carry nothing away
kicker: Psalm 49 · Be not thou afraid
scripture: Psalm 49:16-18 | mark: For when he dieth he shall carry nothing away
p: David laments the fact that worldly prosperity deceives the wealthy, their own riches fooling them into a false sense of security. This is the larger context of the preaching of Christ;

--- The eye of a needle
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 49:16–17
scripture: Matthew 19:23-26 | mark: with God all things are possible
p: The only hope of man, rich or poor, is God. Only in Him is salvation possible.
big: Proverbs 15:16 | mark: Better is little with the fear of the LORD

--- Neither poverty nor riches
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 49:16–17
p: The blessings of this life are not a hope for the next. The praise of men is vanity before the Throne of an almighty and holy God.
p: Agur's prayer in **Proverbs** directly addresses this wisdom;
scripture: Proverbs 30:8-9 | mark: give me neither poverty nor riches
p: Better to be satisfied with what the Lord has given us, and trust and fear Him, than to allow wealth to take us from fearing the Lord, or poverty that brings bitterness to our soul.

--- God will redeem my soul
kicker: Psalm 49 · From the power of the grave
p: **Psalm 49** perfectly encapsulates these wise sayings, showing that David's hope is not in riches, but in the salvation of the Lord.
big: Psalm 49:15 | mark: But God will redeem my soul from the power of the grave
p: So, no matter the station you have been born to, or the prosperity you've been blessed with, fear God and follow His ways, seeking His wisdom and be blessed by His redemption. Learn from his dark sayings and parables, before it is too late.

--- Man that is in honour
kicker: Psalm 49 · Understandeth not
scripture: Psalm 49:19-20 | mark: Man that is in honour, and understandeth not, is like the beasts that perish

=== Dark sayings made plain
--- I will open my mouth in parables
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 49:4
p: The call by David to hear and understand his "dark sayings and parables," brings to mind the way Jesus taught in His ministry.
scripture: Matthew 13:34-35 | mark: I will open my mouth in parables

--- A spiritual filter
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 49:4
p: Jesus revealed to His disciples that parables act as a spiritual filter. To the humble seeker of wisdom, they provide deeper understanding; to the spiritually blind, they remain obscure "dark sayings."
scripture: Matthew 13:10-13 | mark: it is given unto you to know the mysteries of the kingdom of heaven

--- I shall shew you plainly
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 49:4
p: The unfolding revelation to man throughout scripture was never meant to keep wisdom hidden. Especially, not forever.
p: Unlike the religions of man, with their esoteric knowledge, vague and shallow sayings fed in dribs and drabs, with no clarity ever given, God's word opens itself up, and Jesus ultimately shows what all these "dark sayings" and prophetic parables actually mean.
big: John 16:25 | mark: I shall shew you plainly of the Father

--- He expounded all things
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 49:4
p: During His earthly ministry, even Jesus' most faithful disciples required His explanation of the parables He spoke in, to the crowds and Pharisees.
scripture: Mark 4:33-34 | mark: he expounded all things to his disciples
p: But the full revelation of scripture came in the days following His resurrection, when all things were finally made known.

--- And in the psalms, concerning me
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 49:4
p: To the men He met on the way, in **Luke 24**, Jesus, *beginning at Moses and all the prophets, he expounded unto them in all the scriptures the things concerning himself*.
p: And to the gathering of the disciples, He did the same;
scripture: Luke 24:44-45 | mark: and in the psalms, concerning me; Then opened he their understanding

--- The words of eternal life
kicker: Application • The scriptures
p: He explicitly references the three divisions of the Old Testament, (the Law, the Prophets, and the Psalms, like Psalm 49) and grants them the spiritually supernatural ability to finally see past the "dark sayings" to understand the plain truth of the Gospel.
p: These things are revealed to us by the Spirit, Who teaches us all things. We are able to see the entire picture, fully revealed in Christ, and the understanding imparted by the Apostles in the Epistles for added clarity and depth. Only the future Revelation of Christ Jesus is still through a glass darkly, (although the gist is pretty clear on timing and events).
p: We see that the Old Testament scriptures aren't an empty recitation of genealogy, geography and events. They aren't the vain contemplations of man. They are the words of eternal life, and all these were written to point the way to Jesus, and provide us with understanding.

--- For our admonition
kicker: Application • The scriptures
big: 1 Corinthians 10:11 | mark: they are written for our admonition
p: Not only does God's word exist to show us His ways, but ultimately it is the tool He has given us to fulfill the Great Commission, leading others to the truth.

--- Ye are witnesses
kicker: Application • The Great Commission
scripture: Luke 24:46-48 | mark: And ye are witnesses of these things
p: We are witnesses of these things. We have the testimony of God, revealed. We are given the wisdom and power of His word to teach repentance towards God and remission of sins by His Son, in Jesus' name.
p: Not in dark sayings and parables, although they help reveal the truth, but by the power of our witness that all is revealed in Christ. Amen.

